(1374316, 170513, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi CookieJar...<br/> <br/>This photo would be much stronger if your subject was sharp.  It''s also a little underexposed.  I can''t really offer you much on this image.  The composition seems to be lacking punch.<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi CookieJar... This photo would be much stronger if your subject was sharp.  It''s also a little underexposed.  I can''t really offer you much on this image.  The composition seems to be lacking punch. John Setzler', '2005-05-09 23:20:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1374538, 170809, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Wow.  Seems like everyone commented on  your title.  If you would have titled it ''the bench'' no one would have even cared about the title. lol<br/>Who cares what you call the pic anyway, you could have titled it ''bird on a barn roof'' for all I care and it would still be a good image.  On that note though, if you DID mean for the boat to be the main focus, I think you failed.  The bench IS the main focus here and the boat adds to it in my opinion.<br/>Focus and clarity are really good.  I like how we can see all the little branches in the trees.<br/>Lighting is also good.  I like how the trees and the bench are silhouetted.  That looks really nice against the blue background.  <br/>You did a wonderful job of getting your horizon horizontal.  i like the combination of the horizontal water line and the diagonal shore line.<br/>The only thing I think I would like to have seen differently, is that I wish there were some clouds in the sky, but for a challenge like minimalism, it''s probably good that there are no clouds...but outside the challenge, I think it would look nice with clouds.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club* Wow.  Seems like everyone commented on  your title.  If you would have titled it ''the bench'' no one would have even cared about the title. lolWho cares what you call the pic anyway, you could have titled it ''bird on a barn roof'' for all I care and it would still be a good image.  On that note though, if you DID mean for the boat to be the main focus, I think you failed.  The bench IS the main focus here and the boat adds to it in my opinion.Focus and clarity are really good.  I like how we can see all the little branches in the trees.Lighting is also good.  I like how the trees and the bench are silhouetted.  That looks really nice against the blue background.  You did a wonderful job of getting your horizon horizontal.  i like the combination of the horizontal water line and the diagonal shore line.The only thing I think I would like to have seen differently, is that I wish there were some clouds in the sky, but for a challenge like minimalism, it''s probably good that there are no clouds...but outside the challenge, I think it would look nice with clouds.~Heather~', '2005-05-10 01:15:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(1374598, 170833, 34103, '<td valign="top">Hello, greetings from the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>Thank you for entering the DPChallenge, Minimalism. <br/> <br/>This is a interesting take on the mininalism challenge. I see the inner workings of a computer. There are many parts, tubes, transistors, circuits, etc. Certainly the inside components of a computer are small and they do add to the total piece of the working puzzle. So I guess you are trying to focus in on the small parts in the bigger picture. <br/> <br/>Initially looking at your image, it is hard to figure out where you appeal is. There really is not a main focal point to view. The message is there, but it is very hard to draw out. The title is a clue to your message, but the details are not clear. <br/> <br/>The blue lighting is a good touch. It almost makes the hardware softer and moody. At the same time there is a lot of light blue constrast on the some of the metal bolt parts. This and the out of focus parts of the computer create a visual distraction. <br/> <br/>Your depth of field could have been better. A lot of foreground is out of focus. What might have worked better, was to have a sharper image of the background as well. It seems that the smaller, tiny components could be classified as minimal. <br/> <br/>Techicallly, there are several factors to address. The focusing, lighting, and subject matter. <br/> <br/>1) Lighting seems to only provide a blue glow, it is not bringing out any of the highlights of the photo. <br/>2) Much of the image is out of focus. I wonder how you even got the camera in that small space. Check your computer/image output when saving for this challenge. Try and keep as close to 150K as possible. <br/>3)There might be some voters who might be wondering, "did this photo meet the challenge. Like where is the minimalism?<br/> <br/>Overall it was a a brave attempt at presenting the minimalism challenge. Work on your subject content. Try different lighting resources.</td>', 'Hello, greetings from the Critique Club.  Thank you for entering the DPChallenge, Minimalism.  This is a interesting take on the mininalism challenge. I see the inner workings of a computer. There are many parts, tubes, transistors, circuits, etc. Certainly the inside components of a computer are small and they do add to the total piece of the working puzzle. So I guess you are trying to focus in on the small parts in the bigger picture.  Initially looking at your image, it is hard to figure out where you appeal is. There really is not a main focal point to view. The message is there, but it is very hard to draw out. The title is a clue to your message, but the details are not clear.  The blue lighting is a good touch. It almost makes the hardware softer and moody. At the same time there is a lot of light blue constrast on the some of the metal bolt parts. This and the out of focus parts of the computer create a visual distraction.  Your depth of field could have been better. A lot of foreground is out of focus. What might have worked better, was to have a sharper image of the background as well. It seems that the smaller, tiny components could be classified as minimal.  Techicallly, there are several factors to address. The focusing, lighting, and subject matter.  1) Lighting seems to only provide a blue glow, it is not bringing out any of the highlights of the photo. 2) Much of the image is out of focus. I wonder how you even got the camera in that small space. Check your computer/image output when saving for this challenge. Try and keep as close to 150K as possible. 3)There might be some voters who might be wondering, "did this photo meet the challenge. Like where is the minimalism? Overall it was a a brave attempt at presenting the minimalism challenge. Work on your subject content. Try different lighting resources.', '2005-05-10 02:05:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1374611, 171293, 14012, '<td valign="top">**Critique Club**<br/> <br/>Hello Heidi -<br/> <br/>I haven''t done one of these for awhile, so here goes.<br/> <br/>Reading through the comments you received, I think everyone offered you a lot of good advice. Here are a couple comments to go along with what has already been said<br/> <br/>The idea is good, b&amp;w is a good choice, but you have to be careful of overexposing your highlights. I recommend reading about The Zone System that Ansel Adams developed. The jest is, in a b&amp;w photo, you will have a range from pure white to pure black with minimal loss of details in the highlights and shadows. Do a Google search on the Zone System and you will find lots of information.<br/> <br/>Like someone mentioned below, a different perspective would have helped. The background is definately a disctraction. Maybe if you put the pawn at one end of the board and then shot down onto the piece at a slight angle, filling the frame with the board it would have helped.<br/> <br/>Just some ideas/suggestions. I hope you find them helpful.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Chris</td>', '**Critique Club** Hello Heidi - I haven''t done one of these for awhile, so here goes. Reading through the comments you received, I think everyone offered you a lot of good advice. Here are a couple comments to go along with what has already been said The idea is good, b&w is a good choice, but you have to be careful of overexposing your highlights. I recommend reading about The Zone System that Ansel Adams developed. The jest is, in a b&w photo, you will have a range from pure white to pure black with minimal loss of details in the highlights and shadows. Do a Google search on the Zone System and you will find lots of information. Like someone mentioned below, a different perspective would have helped. The background is definately a disctraction. Maybe if you put the pawn at one end of the board and then shot down onto the piece at a slight angle, filling the frame with the board it would have helped. Just some ideas/suggestions. I hope you find them helpful. Regards,Chris', '2005-05-10 02:20:13.000000', NULL, 0),
